1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:08,880
We've seen the valuations of a bunch of software companies crash because people are expecting AI to commoditize software. There's a potentially naive way of thinking

2
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:19,440
about things, which is: look, Nvidia sends a GDS2 file to TSMC. TSMC builds the logic dies, it builds the switches, then it packages them with the HBM

3
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:27,440
that SK Hynix, Micron, and Samsung make. Then it sends it to an ODM in Taiwan where they assemble the racks. Nvidia is fundamentally making software that

4
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:46,160
other people are manufacturing, and if software gets commoditized, does Nvidia get commoditized? In the end, something has to transform electrons to tokens. The transformation of electrons to tokens

5
00:00:46,160 --> 00:01:05,519
and making those tokens more valuable over time is hard to completely commoditize. The transformation from electrons to tokens is such an incredible journey.

6
00:01:05,519 --> 00:01:16,240
Making that token is like making one molecule more valuable than another molecule, making one token more valuable than another. The amount of artistry, engineering,

7
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:30,080
science, and invention that goes into making that token valuable, obviously we're watching it happen in real time. The transformation, the manufacturing, all of the

8
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:46,240
science that goes in there is far from deeply understood and the journey is far from over. I doubt that it will happen. We're going to make it more efficient, of course.

9
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:58,560
The way that you framed the question is my mental model of our company. The input is electrons, the output is tokens. In the middle is Nvidia. Our job

10
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:10,720
is to do as much as necessary and as little as possible to enable that transformation to be done at incredible capabilities. What I mean by "as little as possible,"

11
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:20,800
whatever I don't need to do, I partner with somebody and make it part of my ecosystem. If you look at Nvidia today, we probably have the largest ecosystem of partners,

12
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:34,160
both in the supply chain upstream and downstream, all of the computer companies, application developers, and model makers. AI is a five-layer cake, if you will.

13
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:46,560
We have ecosystems across the entire five layers. We try to do as little as possible, but the part that we have to do, as it turns out, is insanely hard.

14
00:02:46,560 --> 00:03:00,320
I don't think that gets commoditized. In fact, I also don't think the enterprise software companies, the tools makers… Most software companies today are tool makers.

15
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:13,840
Some of them are not. Some of them are workflow codification systems. But for a lot of companies, they're tool makers. For example, Excel is a tool, PowerPoint is a

16
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:27,200
tool, Cadence makes tools, Synopsys makes tools. I actually see the opposite of what people see. I think the number of agents is going to grow exponentially, and the number

17
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:42,400
of tool users is going to grow exponentially. It's very likely that the number of instances of all these tools is going to skyrocket. It’s very likely that the number of instances

18
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:58,080
of Synopsys Design Compiler is going to skyrocket, along with the number of agents using the floor planners, our layout tools, and our design rule checkers.

19
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:04,320
Today we're limited by the number of engineers. Tomorrow, those engineers are going to be supported by a bunch of agents. We're going to be exploring the

20
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:14,560
design space like you've never seen before, and we're going to use the tools that we use today. I think tool use is going to cause the software companies to skyrocket.

21
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:21,519
The reason why it hasn't happened yet is because the agents aren't good enough at using their tools yet. Either these companies are going to build

22
00:04:21,519 --> 00:04:32,480
the agents themselves, or agents are going to get good enough to be able to use those tools. I think it's going to be a combination of both. I think in your latest filings, you had almost

23
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:44,880
a $100 billion in purchase commitments with foundries, memory, and packaging. SemiAnalysis has reported that you will have $250 billion of these kinds of purchase commitments.

24
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:54,160
One interpretation is that Nvidia's moat is really that you've locked up many years of these scarce components. Somebody else might have an accelerator,

25
00:04:54,160 --> 00:05:01,920
but can they actually get the memory to build it? Can they actually get the logic to build it? Is this really Nvidia's big moat for the next few years?

26
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:14,640
It's one of the things that we can do that is hard for someone else to do. We've made enormous commitments upstream. Some of it is explicit, these commitments that you mentioned.

27
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:27,440
Some of it is implicit. For example, a lot of the investments that are upstream are made by our supply chain because I said to the CEOs, "Let me tell you how big this industry is going to be,

28
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:44,320
let me explain to you why, let me reason through it with you, and let me show you what I see." As a result of that process of informing, inspiring, and aligning with CEOs of all

29
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:51,120
different industries upstream, they're willing to make the investments. Why are they willing to make the investments for me and not someone else?

30
00:05:51,120 --> 00:06:02,880
The reason for that is because they know that I have the capacity to buy their supply and sell it through my downstream. The fact is that Nvidia's downstream supply

31
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:18,720
chain and our downstream demand is so large, they're willing to make the investment upstream. If you look at GTC, people are marveled by the scale of it and the people that go.

32
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:31,200
It's a full 360 degrees, the entire universe of AI all in one place. They're all in one place because they need to see each other. I bring them together so that the downstream can see the upstream,

33
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:42,320
the upstream can see the downstream, and all of them can see the advances in AI. Very importantly, they can all meet the AI natives, all the AI startups being built,

34
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:55,040
and all the amazing things happening so they can see firsthand all the things that I tell them. I spend a lot of my time informing, directly or indirectly, our supply chain, partners, and

35
00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:15,440
ecosystem about the opportunity in front of us. Some people always say, "Jensen, in most keynotes, it's one announcement after another." With our keynotes, there’s always a part

36
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:27,360
of it that's a little torturous in the sense that it almost comes across like education. In fact, that's exactly on my mind. I need to make sure the entire supply

37
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:40,400
chain, upstream and downstream, the ecosystem, understands what is coming at us, why it's coming, when it's coming, how big it's going to be, and is able to reason about it systematically,

38
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:56,240
just like I reason about it. Regarding the moat as you describe it, we're able to build for a future. If our next several years are a trillion dollars

39
00:07:56,240 --> 00:08:10,000
in scale, we have the supply chain to do it. Without our reach, the velocity of our business… Just as there's cash flow, there's supply chain flow, there's churns.

40
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:23,120
Nobody is going to build a supply chain for an architecture if the business churns are low. Our ability to sustain the scale is only because our downstream demand is so great.

41
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:32,240
And they see it, they hear about it, they see it all coming. That allows us to do the things we're able to do at the scale we do them.

42
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:41,280
I do want to understand more concretely whether the upstream can keep up. For many years now, you guys have been 2x-ing revenue year over year.

43
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:49,920
You've been more than tripling the amount of flops you're providing to the world year over year. And 2x-ing at this scale now is really incredible. Exactly. But then you look at logic.

44
00:08:49,920 --> 00:09:00,320
You're the biggest customer on TSMC's N3 node, and you're one of the biggest on N2. AI as a whole this year is going to be sixty percent of N3.

45
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:09,680
It's going to be 86% next year, according to SemiAnalysis. How do you double if you're the majority? And how do you do that year over year?

46
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:25,920
Are we in a regime now where the growth rate in AI compute has to slow because of upstream? Do you see a way to get around this? How do we build 2x more fabs year over year, ultimately? At some level,

47
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:46,160
the instantaneous demand is greater than the supply upstream and downstream in the world. At any instant, we could be limited by the number of plumbers, which actually happens.

48
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:59,680
The plumbers are invited to next year's GTC. By the way, great idea. But that's a good condition. You want an industry where the instantaneous demand is greater

49
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:15,440
than the total supply of the industry. The opposite is obviously less good. If we're too far apart, if one particular component is too far away, the industry swarms it.

50
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:25,040
For example, notice people aren't talking very much about CoWoS anymore. The reason for that is because for two years we swarmed the living daylights out of it.

51
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:36,000
We doubled, doubled, doubled on several doubles. Now I think we're in fairly good shape. TSMC now knows that CoWoS supply has to keep up with the rest of the logic demand

52
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:46,720
and the memory demand. They're scaling CoWoS and future packaging technologies at the same level as they scale logic.

53
00:10:46,720 --> 00:11:01,280
This is terrific, because for a long time, CoWoS and HBM memory were rather specialty. But they're not specialties anymore. People now realize they're mainstream computing technology.

54
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:15,280
Of course, we're now much more able to influence a larger scope of our supply chain. At the beginning of the AI revolution, all the things

55
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:26,880
that I say now, I was saying five years ago. Some people believed in it and invested in it, for example, Sanjay and the Micron team. I still remember the meeting really well

56
00:11:26,880 --> 00:11:38,480
where I was clear about exactly what was going to happen, why it was going to happen, and the predictions of today. They really doubled down on it.

57
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:56,960
We partnered with them across LPDDR and HBM memories, and they really invested in it. It obviously has been tremendous for the company. Some people came a little bit later, but now they're all here.

58
00:11:56,960 --> 00:12:13,120
Each one of these bottlenecks gets a great deal of attention. Now we're prefetching the bottlenecks years in advance. For example, the investments that we've done with Lumentum, Coherent,

59
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:30,320
and the silicon photonics ecosystem over the last several years really reshaped the supply chain. We built up an entire supply chain around TSMC. We partnered with them on COUPE, invented a whole

60
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:42,560
bunch of technology, and licensed those patents to the supply chain to keep it nice and open. We're preparing the supply chain through the invention of new technologies, new workflows,

61
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:52,320
new testing equipment like double-sided probing, investing in companies, and helping them scale up their capacity. You can see that we're trying to

62
00:12:52,320 --> 00:13:00,560
shape the ecosystem so that the supply chain is ready to support the scale. It seems like some bottlenecks are easier than others.

63
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:14,240
Scaling up CoWoS versus scaling up— I went to the hardest one, by the way. Which is? Plumbers. Plumbers and electricians. This is

64
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:29,520
one of the concerns that I have about the doomers describing the end of work and killing of jobs. If we discourage people from being software engineers,

65
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:43,040
we're going to run out of software engineers. The same prediction happened ten years ago. Some of the doomers were telling people, "Whatever you do, don't be a radiologist."

66
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:51,520
You might hear some of those videos still on the web saying radiology is going to be the first career to go and the world is not going to need any more radiologists.

67
00:13:51,520 --> 00:14:03,520
Guess what we're short of? Radiologists. Going back to this point about how some things you can scale, and other things… How do you actually manufacture 2x the amount of logic a year?

68
00:14:03,520 --> 00:14:17,200
Ultimately, memory and logic are bottlenecked by EUV. How do you get to 2x as many EUV machines year over year? None of that is impossible to scale quickly. All of that is easy to do

69
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:28,080
within two or three years. You just need a demand signal. Once you can build one, you can build ten, and once you can build ten, you can build a million.

70
00:14:28,080 --> 00:14:36,480
These things are not hard to replicate. How far down the supply chain do you go? Do you go to ASML and say, "Hey, if I look out three years from now,

71
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:46,800
for Nvidia to be generating two trillion a year in revenue, we need way more EUV machines"? Some of them I have to directly, some of them indirectly, and some of them…

72
00:14:46,800 --> 00:15:04,240
If I can convince TSMC, ASML will be convinced. We have to think about the critical pinch points. But if TSMC is convinced, you'll have plenty of EUV machines in a few years.

73
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:12,160
My point is that none of the bottlenecks last longer than a couple of years, two, three years, none of them. Meanwhile, we're

74
00:15:12,160 --> 00:15:24,480
improving computing efficiency by 10x 20x, and in the case of Hopper to Blackwell, 30x to 50x. We're coming up with new algorithms because CUDA is so flexible.

75
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:36,400
We're developing all kinds of new techniques so that we drive efficiency in addition to increasing capacity. None of those things worry me.

76
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:48,880
It's the stuff that's downstream from us. Energy policies that prevent energy from… You can't create an industry without energy. You can't create a whole new

77
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:58,000
manufacturing industry without energy. We want to reindustrialize the United States. We want to bring back chip manufacturing, computer manufacturing, and packaging.

78
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:08,560
We want to build new things like EVs and robots. We want to build AI factories. You can't build any of these things without energy, and those things take a long time.

79
00:16:08,560 --> 00:16:17,680
More chip capacity, that's a 2-3 year problem. More CoWoS capacity, 2-3 year problem. Interesting. I feel like I have guests tell me the exact opposite thing sometimes.

80
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:23,280
In this case, I just don't have the technical knowledge to adjudicate. The beautiful thing is you're talking to the expert.

81
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:39,200
True. I want to ask about your competitors. If you look at the TPU, arguably two out of the top three models in the world, Claude and Gemini, were trained on TPU.

82
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:56,160
What does that mean for Nvidia going forward? We build a very different thing. What Nvidia built is accelerated computing, not a tensor processing unit.

83
00:16:56,160 --> 00:17:09,200
Accelerated computing is used for all kinds of things: molecular dynamics, quantum chromodynamics, data processing, data frames, structured data, and unstructured data.

84
00:17:09,200 --> 00:17:22,640
It's also used for fluid dynamics and particle physics. In addition, we use it for AI. Accelerated computing is much more diverse.

85
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:34,960
Although AI is the conversation today and is obviously very important and impactful, computing is much broader than that. Nvidia has reinvented the way computing

86
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:47,040
is done, moving from general-purpose computing to accelerated computing. Our market reach is far greater than any TPU or ASIC can possibly have.

87
00:17:47,040 --> 00:18:02,560
If you look at our position, we're the only company that accelerates applications of all kinds. We have a gigantic ecosystem. So all kinds of frameworks and algorithms run on Nvidia.

88
00:18:02,560 --> 00:18:16,480
Because our computers are designed to be operated by other people, anyone who's an operator can buy our systems. With most of these home-built systems,

89
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:25,680
you have to be your own operator because they were never designed to be flexible enough for others to operate. Because anybody can operate our systems,

90
00:18:25,680 --> 00:18:40,640
we're in every cloud, including Google, Amazon, Azure, and OCI. If you want to operate it to rent, you better have a large ecosystem of customers

91
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:55,120
in many industries to be the offtakers. If you want to operate it for yourself, we obviously have the ability to help you operate it yourself, like we did for Elon with xAI.

92
00:18:55,120 --> 00:19:10,720
And because we can enable operators in any company and any industry, you could use it to build a supercomputer for scientific research and drug discovery at Lilly.

93
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:21,760
We can help them operate their own supercomputer and use it for the entire diversity of drug discovery and biological sciences that we accelerate.

94
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:34,160
There are just a whole bunch of applications that we can address that you can't do with TPUs. Nvidia built CUDA to be a fantastic tensor processing unit as well,

95
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:48,240
but it also handles every life cycle of data processing, computing, AI, and so on. Our market opportunity is just a lot larger, and our reach is a lot greater.

96
00:19:48,240 --> 00:20:00,160
Because we support every application in the world now, you can build Nvidia systems anywhere and know that there will be customers for it. It's a very different thing.

97
00:20:00,160 --> 00:20:10,800
This is going to be a long question. You have spectacular revenue, and you're not making $60 billion a quarter from pharma and quantum.

98
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:19,360
You're making it because AI is an unprecedented technology that is growing unprecedentedly fast. The question then is what is best for AI specifically.

99
00:20:19,360 --> 00:20:27,440
I'm not in the details, but I talk to my AI researcher friends and they say, "Look, when I use a TPU, it's this big systolic array that's perfect for doing matrix multiplies,

100
00:20:27,440 --> 00:20:38,880
whereas a GPU is very flexible. It's great when you have lots of branching or irregular memory access." But what is AI? It's just these very predictable matrix

101
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:47,360
multiplies again and again and again. You don't have to give up any die area for warp schedulers or switches between threads and memory banks.

102
00:20:47,360 --> 00:21:01,200
And the TPU is really optimized for the bulk of this growth in revenue and use case for compute that is coming online right now. I wonder how you react to that.

103
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:13,920
Matrix multiplies are an important part of AI, but they're not the only part. If you want to come up with a new attention mechanism, disaggregate in a different way,

104
00:21:13,920 --> 00:21:29,680
or invent a whole new type of architecture altogether—like a hybrid SSM—you want an architecture that's generally programmable. If you want to create a model that fuses diffusion

105
00:21:29,680 --> 00:21:44,560
and autoregressive techniques, you want an architecture that’s just generally programmable. We run everything you can imagine. That's the advantage. It allows for the invention

106
00:21:44,560 --> 00:22:00,640
of new algorithms a lot more easily, because it's a programmable system. The ability to invent new algorithms is really what makes AI advance so quickly.

107
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:15,360
TPUs, like anything else, are impacted by Moore's Law, which we know is increasing by about 25% per year. The only way to really get 10x or 100x leaps

108
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:33,920
is to fundamentally change the algorithm and how it's computed every single year. That's Nvidia's fundamental advantage. The only reason we were able to make Blackwell to Hopper 50x… When I

109
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:49,360
first announced Blackwell was going to be 35x more energy efficient than Hopper, nobody believed it. Then Dylan wrote an article saying I sandbagged, and it's actually fifty times.

110
00:22:49,360 --> 00:23:02,240
You can't reasonably do that with just Moore's Law. The way we solve that problem is with new models, like MoEs, that are parallelized, disaggregated,

111
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:15,520
and distributed across a computing system. Without the ability to really get down and come up with new kernels with CUDA, it's really hard to do.

112
00:23:15,520 --> 00:23:28,720
It's the combination of the programmability of our architecture and the fact that Nvidia is an extreme co-design company. We can even offload some of the

113
00:23:28,720 --> 00:23:45,200
computation into the fabric itself, like NVLink, or into the network with Spectrum-X. We could affect change across the processors, the system, the fabric, the libraries, and the

114
00:23:45,200 --> 00:23:58,320
algorithm simultaneously. Without CUDA to do that, I wouldn't even know where to start. My sponsor Crusoe was among the first clouds to offer NVIDIA’s Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra platforms.

115
00:23:58,320 --> 00:24:05,520
And they just announced their NVIDIA Vera Rubin deployment scheduled for later this year. But access to state-of-the-art hardware is only part of the story.

116
00:24:05,520 --> 00:24:13,600
For example, most inference engines already do KV caching for a single user's forward passes. But Crusoe does it across users and GPUs. So if a thousand agents are running on

117
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:21,840
the same system prompt, Crusoe only has to compute the KV cache once for it to become available to every single GPU in the cluster. This is especially important as systems get more

118
00:24:21,840 --> 00:24:32,640
agentic and require much longer prefixes in order to use tools and access files. In a recent benchmark, Crusoe was able to deliver up to 10x faster time-to-first token

119
00:24:32,640 --> 00:24:40,800
and up to 5x better throughput than vLLM. This is just one among many reasons that you should run your inference workload with Crusoe. And if you need GPUs for training,

120
00:24:40,800 --> 00:24:50,960
you don't need to switch clouds. Crusoe's got you covered there too. Go to crusoe.ai/dwarkesh to learn more. This gets at an interesting question about

121
00:24:50,960 --> 00:25:08,160
Nvidia's clientele. 60% of your revenue is coming from these big five hyperscalers. In a different era with different customers—let's say professors running experiments—they need CUDA.

122
00:25:08,160 --> 00:25:15,120
They can't use another accelerator. They just needed to run PyTorch with CUDA and have everything optimized. But these hyperscalers have

123
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:23,360
the resources to write their own kernels. In fact, they have to in order to get that last 5% of performance they need for their specific architecture.

124
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:38,400
Anthropic and Google are mostly running their own accelerators or running TPUs and Trainium. But even OpenAI, using GPUs, has Triton because they need their own kernels.

125
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:51,440
Down to CUDA C++, instead of using cuBLAS and NCCL, they've got their own stack which compiles to other accelerators as well. If most of your customers can and do make

126
00:25:51,440 --> 00:26:04,560
replacements for CUDA, to what extent is CUDA really the thing that is going to make frontier AI happen on Nvidia? CUDA is a rich ecosystem. If you want to

127
00:26:04,560 --> 00:26:16,400
build on any computer first, building on CUDA first is incredibly smart. Because the ecosystem is so rich, we support every framework.

128
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:28,320
If you want to create custom kernels… For example, we contribute enormously to Triton. So the back end of Triton has huge amounts of Nvidia technology.

129
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:38,640
We're delighted to help every framework become as great as it can be. There are lots and lots of frameworks. There's Triton, vLLM, SGLang, and more.

130
00:26:38,640 --> 00:26:50,240
Now there's a whole bunch of new reinforcement learning frameworks coming out, like verl and NeMo RL. With post-training and reinforcement

131
00:26:50,240 --> 00:27:00,800
learning, that entire area is just exploding. So if you want to build on an architecture, building on CUDA makes the most sense because you know the ecosystem is great.

132
00:27:00,800 --> 00:27:08,880
You know that if something happens, it's more likely in your code and not in the mountain of code underneath. Don't forget the amount of code you're dealing

133
00:27:08,880 --> 00:27:17,600
with when building these systems. When something doesn't work, was it you or was it the computer? You would like it to always be

134
00:27:17,600 --> 00:27:31,200
you and to be able to trust the computer. Obviously, we still have lots of bugs ourselves, but our system is so well wrung out that you can at least build on top of the foundation.

135
00:27:31,200 --> 00:27:42,240
That's number one: the richness, programmability, and capability of the ecosystem. The second thing is, if you're a developer building anything at all, the single most

136
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:52,160
important thing you want is an install base. You want the software you write to run on a whole bunch of other computers. You're not building software just for yourself.

137
00:27:52,160 --> 00:28:02,240
You're building it for your fleet or everybody else's fleet because you're a framework builder. Nvidia's CUDA ecosystem is ultimately its great treasure.

138
00:28:02,240 --> 00:28:21,840
We have several hundred million GPUs out there now. Every cloud has it. It goes back to the A10, A100, H100, H200, the L series, the P series. There’s a whole bunch of them.

139
00:28:21,840 --> 00:28:32,480
They're in all kinds of sizes and shapes. If you're a robotics company, you want that CUDA stack to actually run in the robot itself. We're literally everywhere. The install base means

140
00:28:32,480 --> 00:28:46,960
that once you develop the software or the model, it's going to be useful everywhere. That is just incredibly valuable. Lastly, the fact that we're in every single cloud makes us genuinely unique.

141
00:28:46,960 --> 00:28:55,440
